.TH LSM 1 "2026-10-29" "lsm 4.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lsm \- Linux system monitor with OpenGL graphs and editable 3D topology
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lsm
.RB [ \-\-view " VIEW"]
.RB [ \-\-root " PATH"]
.br
.B lsm
.RB [ \-\-check " | " \-\-smoke ]
.RB [ \-\-view " VIEW"]
.RB [ \-\-root " PATH"]
.br
.B lsm
.BR \-\-help " | " \-h " | " \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lsm
is a native C Linux system monitor using OpenGL for its interface,
2D histories and 3D graphs.
It displays CPU cores, available GPU telemetry, process activity,
memory accounting, pressure stalls, process ancestry and directory metadata.
Collection runs in background threads independently of rendering.
.PP
Normal operation requires a graphical desktop and an OpenGL compatibility
context. Root privileges are not required to start the program.
Access to individual processes, memory mappings, memory bytes and hardware
counters follows normal Linux permissions.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-view " VIEW"
Open a named view:
.B overview, cpu, gpu, processes, pressure, ram, tree, files, manual.
The default is
.BR overview .
.TP
.BI \-\-root " PATH"
Set the initial filesystem root. The default is the current working directory,
or the filesystem root if the working directory cannot be obtained.
This option does not select the Filesystem view automatically.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Run collector, parser, memory, topology and interaction checks, then exit.
No graphical display is needed. Signal tests use temporary child processes;
byte tests read controlled data in the test process; filesystem tests create
and remove temporary fixtures.
.TP
.B \-\-smoke
Run graphical checks and exit. Tests traverse all views, change the window
size, briefly request fullscreen, and exercise process dialogs, memory
inspection and topology editing. Tests signal only their own temporary
children and use temporary filesystem fixtures.
.TP
.BR \-h , " \-\-help"
Print command-line help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the program name and version and exit.
.SH VIEWS
Click a navigation tab or press its number when no text field, dialog or
inspector is capturing keyboard input.
.TP
.B 1 Overview
Resource summaries, 3D CPU history, memory history and disk throughput.
.TP
.B 2 CPU
Per-logical-CPU activity, clocks, physical topology and available temperatures.
Click a core for its user, system and I/O-wait breakdown.
.TP
.B 3 GPU
Discovered devices, driver and PCI details, engine activity, clocks,
resident memory and available temperature and power counters.
Use the device and graph buttons to change the GPU or 2D/3D view;
scroll to see more telemetry.
.TP
.B 4 Processes
Searchable process table with sortable columns, selected-process details,
inspection, End and Kill controls.
.TP
.B 5 Pressure
CPU, memory and I/O pressure stall information, 10/60/300-second averages
and measured histories. These values describe stalled time.
.TP
.B 6 RAM
System memory categories and largest processes by resident size.
Click a process to inspect its mappings.
.TP
.B 7 Process tree
Live 3D parent/child relationships from observed PPIDs.
Arrowheads point toward children. The synthetic Visible roots node groups
processes whose parents are outside the view.
Edges represent ancestry; IPC, sockets and historical lifetimes are not shown.
.TP
.B 8 Filesystem
Bounded 3D directory metadata snapshot with navigation and visual node editing.
.TP
.B 9 Manual
In-app reference. Scroll with the wheel, arrow keys or Page Up/Down;
Home and End jump to the beginning and end.
.SH GENERAL CONTROLS
.TP
.B P / palette button
Cycle Spectrum, Aurora and Ember resource colors for the current session.
Warning amber and danger red retain their meaning in every palette.
.TP
.B F
Toggle fullscreen.
.TP
.B Space
Freeze or resume the displayed snapshot. Background collection continues.
.TP
.B V
Cycle history windows of 1, 5 and 15 minutes.
The History button performs the same action.
.TP
.B Hover or click a 2D graph
Hover to inspect a measured sample; click to pin or unpin the shared time cursor.
Press C to clear it. The cursor does not rewind process tables,
topology, filesystem snapshots or memory bytes.
.TP
.B Drag a panel divider
Resize graph and detail panels in Overview, GPU and topology views.
.TP
.B Left-drag a CPU/GPU 3D history
Orbit the graph. The wheel or +/\- zooms; R resets its camera.
.TP
.B Escape
Close core details, cancel a confirmation, leave a search or close the inspector first.
In topology, cancel an active gesture or deselect.
Elsewhere, exit the application.
.SH CPU CORE DETAILS
On Overview, hover a 3D CPU trace to read its logical CPU ID, recorded usage
and sample age. Click to select that CPU, pin the sample and open its details.
I toggles the panel; Escape or Close closes it.
In narrow windows the panel replaces the graph until closed.
In CPU view, select a core and use Core details or I.
.PP
Use the < and > buttons, [ and ], or left/right arrows to browse cores.
The wheel over the panel and up/down/Page Up/Down scroll its rows.
CPU view covers all collected IDs, including those outside Overview's
first-128-CPU plot.
.PP
Details include physical core, socket and die IDs, sibling CPU IDs,
raw kernel core type, utilization breakdown, reported clock, hardware clock
range, policy min/max, base clock, governor, driver, available core temperature,
L1 data/instruction and L2/L3 cache sizes, and window sample average/peak/count.
Missing fields are unavailable; missing cache sizes use a question mark.
Cache instances and core sensors may be shared among logical CPUs.
Hardware fields and rate breakdowns are from the latest displayed snapshot.
Historical selection records usage only and does not rewind clocks or temperatures.
.SH PROCESS CONTROLS
In Processes, press / or click the search field to filter by name, command
or PID. Click a column heading to sort and click it again to reverse.
Select a process row before using the following controls.
.TP
.B I
Open or close the process inspector. Inspector keys 1, 2 and 3 select CPU,
RAM and I/O histories; 4 or M opens Maps; 5 or B requests Bytes.
.TP
.B T
Open End, which sends SIGTERM after confirmation.
.TP
.B K
Open Kill, which sends SIGKILL after confirmation.
SIGKILL terminates the process immediately and can lose unsaved work.
.PP
Verify the named target in the dialog. Enter confirms; Escape cancels.
Only the selected process is signaled, not its descendants.
The program checks PID/start-time identity and uses a pidfd to avoid signaling
a different process after PID reuse. PID 1 and lsm itself are protected.
The kernel enforces signal permissions; lsm does not elevate privileges.
.SH MEMORY INSPECTION
Maps shows mapping permissions, resident memory, proportional memory and
mapped names. Cycle the type button to filter regions; S changes sorting
between resident size and address. The wheel scrolls the list.
At most 4,096 regions are listed; summary accounting includes additional regions.
.PP
Select a readable region and request Bytes to read at most 256 bytes from
.IR /proc/PID/mem .
Prev, Next and Reload issue explicit additional requests.
Raw bytes are not refreshed automatically.
Access denial or an exited target is reported without bypassing kernel
debugging permissions.
.PP
RSS includes shared resident pages. PSS apportions them among processes.
System RAM categories overlap and must not be summed as independent totals.
Labels such as heap and stack do not decode application objects.
.SH TOPOLOGY EDITING
These controls apply to Process tree and Filesystem.
.TP
.B Left-click a node or label
Select the visible node. Small nodes have expanded pick targets.
.TP
.B Left-drag a node
Move its visual placement in the plane facing the camera.
.TP
.B Right-drag
Orbit the camera.
.TP
.BR Middle-drag ", Shift+left-drag, or drag empty space"
Pan the camera.
.TP
.B Wheel or +/\-
Zoom.
.TP
.B R / Fit
Fit the camera while keeping node placements.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Z
Undo a visual node placement.
.TP
.B G / Reset
Restore the automatic layout for the current scope.
.TP
.B Escape during a gesture
Cancel the gesture and restore its previous placement or camera.
.PP
Visual edits do not change files or process PPIDs.
Placements are scoped to the process subtree/filter or filesystem root.
Process identities use PID and start time; file identities use full paths.
Background collection continues while the rendered scene is held during a gesture.
Placements, layout settings and undo history are bounded and kept for this session.
.SS Process tree navigation
Enter/Focus isolates the selected subtree; Backspace/Parent focuses its parent.
A/All restores the full forest; U/Mine toggles the current user's processes.
Press / to search a name or PID, then Enter to focus the first match.
L/Follow tracks the selected PID/start-time identity and refits when its family
changes. Manual camera or node gestures and explicit navigation end Follow.
I, T and K provide the process inspector and signal dialogs.
Family CPU is summed; family RSS can count shared pages more than once.
.SS Filesystem navigation
Enter opens the selected directory; Backspace/Up moves to its parent.
The Home and / buttons select those roots.
Press / or click the path field to type a directory, then Enter.
Depth cycles through 1\-4 levels; H toggles hidden entries; Refresh rescans.
.PP
Scans read directory entries, stat metadata and symlink target text.
Child symlinks are displayed without following them; mount points are shown
but not descended into until explicitly entered.
Snapshots contain at most 2,048 entries including the root and report truncation.
Directory sizes are inode sizes, not recursive storage totals.
Metadata may change during a scan; slow filesystems may delay completion.
.SH METRICS AND LIMITS
Samples are collected approximately once per second. Histories retain at most
900 measured samples using monotonic timestamps and fill from live data.
No history is saved across restarts. Process CPU uses 100 percent per logical
CPU and can exceed 100 percent. System memory utilization uses MemAvailable.
.PP
The collector supports up to 1,024 CPU IDs, 32,768 processes and 16 GPUs.
Overview's 3D graph draws the first 128 CPU IDs. Process tree displays at most
4,095 processes plus its grouping node.
.PP
CPU, memory, processes, network, disk and pressure counters come from procfs;
hardware topology, clocks and sensors also use sysfs.
Network totals exclude loopback but may double-count bridges and tunnels.
Disk totals omit loop, RAM, device-mapper and md devices to reduce duplication.
.PP
Intel and AMD engine coverage depends on accessible DRM client counters.
GPU utilization is the busiest visible engine, not a sum of engines.
Client resident-memory sums can double-count shared buffers.
Integrated GPUs share system RAM; no dedicated VRAM total is invented.
NVIDIA uses optional dynamically loaded NVML; this backend does not provide
engine-level splits. GPU discovery occurs at startup.
Unavailable counters are labeled unavailable.
Intel i915 has been tested on the development machine;
AMD, xe and NVIDIA require validation on their hardware.
.SH COLORS AND ALERTS
CPU, core and GPU utilization, system RAM use and dedicated GPU VRAM use
turn amber at 85 percent and red at 95 percent.
Cards, values, bars and measured graph segments share these thresholds.
CPU/RAM/GPU percentage charts shade the warning and danger zones;
CPU/GPU 3D histories mark the 95-percent boundary.
.PP
The header lists readings responsible for WARNING or DANGER ZONE.
SYSTEM OVERLOAD appears in red when the 1-minute load divided by online
logical CPU count reaches 1.5, PSI some avg10 reaches 25 percent, or
memory/I/O PSI full avg10 reaches 15 percent.
Amber thresholds are load/CPU 1.0, PSI some 10 percent and PSI full 5 percent.
Load includes runnable tasks and uninterruptible sleep;
PSI values describe stalled time.
.PP
These are application display thresholds for resource use.
High utilization alone uses DANGER ZONE.
They do not define hardware temperature limits.
Unavailable metrics do not create alerts; swap occupancy alone does not
trigger overload. Process CPU values retain their per-logical-CPU units,
while their warning colors use total machine CPU capacity.
HELD identifies alerts from frozen snapshots or scenes held during editing.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MONITOR_FONT
Path to a monospace TrueType font. Otherwise lsm tries DejaVu Sans Mono,
then falls back to FreeGLUT's built-in font.
.TP
.B LSM_CAPTURE_DIR
Existing writable directory for PPM screenshots during
.BR \-\-smoke .
Files are named with the lsm prefix. Ignored in normal operation.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /proc
Linux runtime counters, process identities, mappings and memory interfaces.
.TP
.I /sys
Hardware, DRM, CPU topology and sensor interfaces.
.TP
.I libnvidia-ml.so.1
Optional NVIDIA runtime library, loaded when available.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal exit, successful checks, help or version.
.TP
.B 1
Collector startup failure, failed checks or detected rendering failure.
The graphics library may also terminate on display/context startup errors.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line arguments.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
lsm
lsm \-\-view tree
lsm \-\-view files \-\-root /home
lsm \-\-view manual
lsm \-\-check
man \-l ./lsm.1
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR top (1),
.BR ps (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR proc (5),
.BR pidfd_send_signal (2)
